{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining right-skewness as a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes. However, in this decision problem, Lottery A has a 33% chance of 2500 Euros (a higher outcome), 66% chance of 2400 Euros (a lower but still positive outcome), and 1% chance of 0 Euros (an unfavorable outcome). Lottery B offers 2400 Euros with 100% probability. The probability distribution of A is not right-skewed; rather, it has a higher probability (66%) on a favorable outcome (2400), and only a small (1%) chance on the worst outcome (0). The distribution of A is actually left-skewed (more weight on the lower end) or at least not right-skewed. In contrast, Lottery B is perfectly symmetric in outcome (no variability), so it is not right-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason, which prefers the more right-skewed lottery, cannot apply here because neither lottery is right-skewed. Thus, the reason is not applicable to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Lotterie A gibt mir mehr Wahrscheinlichkeiten auch ein größeren Gewinn zu machen als in Lotterie B denn da hab ich nur eine Wahrscheinlichkeit.' This translates to: 'Lottery A gives me more probabilities and also a greater chance to win a larger amount than in Lottery B because there I only have one probability.' The individual is emphasizing that Lottery A has multiple outcomes (more probabilities) and a chance to win 2500 Euros, which is higher than the 2400 Euros in Lottery B. The individual does not mention probabilities of unfavorable outcomes, skewness, or any comparison of the distribution's shape. The justification is based on the number of outcomes and the possibility of a higher payoff, not on skewness or the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. Thus, the deliberation process focuses on the potential for a higher gain and the variety of outcomes, not on the skewness of the distributions.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is about skewness, specifically right-skewness (greater probability on unfavorable outcomes), and it prefers the more right-skewed lottery. However, neither lottery is right-skewed: Lottery A has a 66% chance of 2400 Euros (favorable), 33% of 2500 (also favorable), and only 1% of 0 (unfavorable), so the unfavorable outcome is very small. Lottery B is deterministic and has no unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, the relevant information for the decision reason is absent. The individual's reasoning focuses on the number of outcomes and the possibility of a higher win, not on skewness or the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. There is no mention of unfavorable outcomes or probability distribution shape. Hence, the decision reason was not used. The individual did not consider or reference skewness in any way. Thus, the decision reason was not applied.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

