  "step_2": "The individual in the verbal report compares the percentages: they note that Lottery A has a larger percentage (0.02%) than Lottery B, though they are uncertain about whether B has 0.1% or more due to the lack of a zero after the decimal. They state that it was more difficult to decide because in both cases, one would mostly lose (i.e., get 0 Euros). They conclude that Lottery A was not as risky as Lottery B. The individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution, or any concept related to the shape of the probability mass (e.g., whether one outcome dominates). Instead, the reasoning centers on perceived risk and the size of the favorable outcome (3000 vs. 6000), but without referencing skewness or the distribution of probabilities. The justification is based on subjective risk perception and the idea that A has a higher chance of a positive outcome (implied by the 0.02% mention), which leads to a lower perceived risk. There is no explicit or implicit reference to skewness or the idea that a larger portion of probability is on unfavorable outcomes (0 Euros).",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the distribution of probabilities or the shape of the outcomes. Instead, they focus on the numerical values of the probabilities and the perceived risk, suggesting that A has a higher chance of winning (0.02% vs. 0.1%), which implies a lower perceived risk. However, the decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, the extent to which the probability mass is concentrated on the unfavorable (0 Euro) outcome. In this case, Lottery B has a smaller favorable probability (0.1%) than A (0.2%), so it is more right-skewed. The individual does not recognize or reference this. Furthermore, their justification does not align with the rule of the decision reason (preferring the more right-skewed lottery). Instead, they justify choosing A due to lower perceived risk, which is inconsistent with preferring a more right-skewed lottery (B). Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual's reasoning is based on different criteria (perceived risk, favorable probability size), not on skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not applied.",
